<header><p class="st-kicker">other · Egypt, North Africa and Sudan</p><h1 class="st-title">Mauretania Caesariensis</h1><p class="st-meta">42 CE – 429 CE (approx.) · capital Caesarea</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The Roman province of western and central Algeria, governed from Juba II's former capital at Caesarea.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/claudius">Claudius</a> created Mauretania Caesariensis in 42 CE from the eastern part of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-mauretania">Kingdom of Mauretania</a>, after the murder of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ptolemy-of-mauretania">King Ptolemy</a>. Its capital was <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/lordship-of-caesarea">Caesarea</a> (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cherchell">Cherchell</a>), the former royal city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juba-ii">Juba II</a>, and it was governed by an equestrian procurator with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/auxilia">auxiliary troops</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Roman towns grew along the coast and fertile valleys, with veteran colonies and estates producing grain and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olive-oil">olive oil</a>, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbers">Berber</a> tribes held the mountains. Frontier forts gradually moved south. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diocletian">Diocletian</a> divided off <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mauretania-sitifensis">Mauretania Sitifensis</a> in the east. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/donatism">Donatist</a> movement, spread widely. The revolts of the Berber princes <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/firmus-berber-prince">Firmus</a> around 372 to 375 and his brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gildo">Gildo</a> showed the power of local dynasts.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vandals">Vandals</a> overran the province from 429, and by treaty in 435 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> conceded control. As Roman authority faded, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbers">Berber</a> leaders such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/masuna">Masuna</a> created the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-the-moors-and-romans">Kingdom of the Moors and Romans</a> in the western part.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/mauretania-caesariensis.jpg" alt="Mauretania Caesariensis" width="320" height="267"><figcaption>Image: Milenioscuro · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Roman_Empire_-_Mauretania_Caesariensis_(125_AD).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>42 CE – 429 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/1st-century">1st century</a> – <a href="/centuries/5th-century">5th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>other</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Caesarea</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Roman religion, Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Latin, Berber, Punic</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/algeria">Algeria</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Caesariensis</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=236&amp;focus=mauretania-caesariensis" aria-label="Mauretania Caesariensis on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4742 828 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Mauretania Caesariensis lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5062 1084L4830 1267M5062 1084L5304 942M5062 1084L5259 1106M5062 1084L4974 1171"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M4830 1267h0M5304 942h0M5259 1106h0M4974 1171h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5062 1084h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5062 1084h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=236&amp;focus=mauretania-caesariensis">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
